* "REL: A Programming language for relational data"
  Wim Martens at SIGMOD'25
  see ====semantic_problems
  * i'm unable to find the talk on direct search
  => nothing open source (formalized, executable). good talk on the problem.
* Ariel: duckdb can be considered an experiment (continued from bigquery, by some of
  the original authors) to bring sanity to SQL by focusing on a subset or
  extending what is needed and doing it to work against CSV or Parquet files
  without needing a real database engine
* about apache datafusion: DataFusion is an extensible query engine written in
  Rust that uses Apache Arrow as its in-memory format.
  Apache Arrow as in-memory format and Apache Parquet as the format for both file
  and network transmission. Duckdb can use both, so can do the latest versions of
  Panda and any really good lib.
  https://datafusion.apache.org/user-guide/sql/struct_coercion.html#null-handling-for-missing-fields
  they override standard SQL with implicit CAST/COERCE where needed so behavior
  is sane.
* Datafusion/Duckdb can also work directly against sqlite, which is more "on
  topic" respect to Arcan. They shine with S3 parqued parquet files and
  interacting with other legacy databases like postgres, today's JDBC.

====ideal_solutions
ORM can hide performance sensitive things like sync, locking and joins while SQL offers no safety-net against such errors besides transactions (but enables more reasoning on perf).
see for example https://travisofthenorth.com/blog/2016/10/2/beware-the-orm
For me I'd be even more radical than ORM (if possible) and version the schema next to build system + application code like how opentofu versions infra
but as generated schema files for migration and restore routines and remove all the bad stuff from SQL + use a streaming language if necessary.
If you can provide me with a non-perf obliterating ORM or non-bullshit SQL linter etc, that would help.
Where ORM help most is to simplify mocking SQL stuff, but with proper libs the data mocking should be also possible to generate from the schema etc.
All I've seen for now is just SQL crimes left and right with non-portable performance hacks on indexing, because everybody knows SQL is only for
non-null portable tables and nobody needs portable and perf-tunable indices into tables.

Looks like dbml might fix the schema part with unclear mocking capabilities.
https://dbml.dbdiagram.io/docs/

SaneSQL linter/lsp is still missing though.
* use case 1: no null fields
* use case 2: mocking based on schema
* use case 3: identify everything non-portable in schema
* use case 4: identify everything non-portable in queries

